@*
    Every colour the game has, for marking an empire with one.

    A tag is one of the game's own flag colours written into the fourth colour slot, which nothing in
    the game reads - see docs/flag-colours.md. So the palette is the game's, drawn in the flag shades
    rather than the map ones: what the reader will see is a dot beside the empire's name in the list,
    and that dot is drawn from the same pair.

    Its own component because three places ask for it - the button on both editors' action rows, and
    the empire's own settings - and a second copy of seventy-two swatches is a second place for the
    "no tag" tile to be forgotten.
*@

<div class="sem-swatches">
    @*
        Taking a tag off is as much a thing to do as putting one on, so it is a tile at the front
        rather than a link beside them.
    *@
    <button type="button" class="sem-swatch none @(Chosen is null ? "selected" : null)"
            title="No tag" aria-label="No tag"
            aria-pressed="@(Chosen is null ? "true" : "false")"
            @onclick="() => Pick(null)"></button>

    @foreach (var color in Session.Data.Database.FlagColors)
    {
        <button type="button"
                class="sem-swatch @(color.Key == Chosen ? "selected" : null)"
                style="background:rgb(@color.Red,@color.Green,@color.Blue)"
                title="@Localizer.Prettify(color.Key)"
                aria-label="@Localizer.Prettify(color.Key)"
                aria-pressed="@(color.Key == Chosen ? "true" : "false")"
                @onclick="() => Pick(color.Key)"></button>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>Handed in rather than cascaded, since one caller sits outside the cascade.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required DesignSession Session { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The colour marked as chosen, or nothing for the empty tile.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Chosen { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised with whatever was pressed, the empty tile raising null.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string?> ChosenChanged { get; set; }

    private Task Pick(string? key) => ChosenChanged.InvokeAsync(key);
}
